Our changes to LSd's source -- its C, and a few of its stock scripts --
applied onto upstream's at image build time (Dockerfile). The lsd submodule itself stays upstream's: make a
change in lsd/, `git -C lsd diff > patches/lsd/NNNN-name.patch`, then
`git -C lsd checkout -- .`. dev/bump-lsd.sh checks every patch still
applies to the new upstream.

  0001-steady-tick.patch  the main loop waits for the next tick in naps
                          of at most 1 ms instead of one sleep, and drops
                          missed ticks past 3 behind instead of running
                          them back to back: a steady 60 ticks a second
                          where the OS wakes sleepers late (Docker
                          Desktop's VM: 16 ms sleeps woke up to 160 ms
                          late, clients saw 10 then 110 updates a
                          second). Worth offering upstream.

  0002-recipients.patch   pid_recipient(): the slots pid_matches() accepts
                          that have an ENet peer. A bot is a subject (damage,
                          piditer) but never a recipient: the loops that send
                          to, or ask has_quirk() of, each slot of a group
                          (send_map, send_spawn_player, send_packet_flags,
                          demand_fingerprint, send_chat, the block-action
                          culls, finish_map_load) walk recipients, so a
                          server of bots no longer calls into Lua for each
                          of them on a block edit or a map change.

  0003-accounts.patch     auth.lua: accounts administered by someone else --
                          useradd <name> <password> [caps...], userpasswd,
                          userdel (passwords redacted from the log, as
                          /register's are) -- and auth_account(name),
                          auth_accounts() to read them. Before, an account
                          could only be made by its player, in game.
                          lsdctl account drives these. Worth offering
                          upstream.

  0004-console-redact.patch  command_log_text(): a command line as it may be
                          logged, a sensitive command's arguments replaced.
                          The socket, websocket and IRC consoles logged each
                          line raw before running it, so a /register or
                          /login typed there put the password in the log.
                          Worth offering upstream.

